asl.library/AbortAslRequest                        asl.library/AbortAslRequest

   NAME
	AbortAslRequest -- A requester up on a process of its own taken down before it is answered.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AbortAslRequest(ab: *AslBase, requester: *anyopaque) void

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -32.

   INPUTS
	- `ab` - asl.library's base.
	- `requester` - what `AllocAslRequest` made, put up with
	  `ASLFR_ReplyPort`.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	The requester's process is given Ctrl-C, which a requester takes as
	being given up: its window closes and its message arrives on its port
	as for any other end, with `answered` 0 - or 1, if the user answered
	it just before. A file requester still reading a drawer stops at the
	next piece of it.

	A requester that is not up - never put up on a port, or ended
	already - is left alone, so a program ending calls this for a
	requester it may have asked with and then waits for the message only
	if one is owed.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: only for the requester's semaphore, held briefly by its
	  process as it ends.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The requester stays the library's until its message has arrived.

   NOTES
	It returns before the requester has gone: the message is what says it
	has. A requester asked and waited for on the caller's own process is
	ended the same way by Ctrl-C to that process.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`AslRequest`, `FreeAslRequest`, exec's `AbortIO`

   EXAMPLES
	    // Ending with a requester up: take it down and wait for its message.
	    if (asking) {
	        ab.AbortAslRequest(req);
	        _ = sys.WaitPort(port);
	        _ = sys.GetMsg(port);
	    }
	    ab.FreeAslRequest(req);
asl.library/AllocAslRequest                        asl.library/AllocAslRequest

   NAME
	AllocAslRequest -- A requester of `kind`, set up from `tags`.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AllocAslRequest(ab: *AslBase, kind: u32, tags: ?[*]const TagItem) ?*anyopaque

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -20.

   INPUTS
	- `ab` - asl.library's base.
	- `kind` - `ASL_FileRequest`, `ASL_FontRequest`,
	  `ASL_ScreenModeRequest` or `ASL_DeviceRequest`.
	- `tags` - what the requester starts as; null for none. `AslRequest`
	  reads the same tags and may change any of them for one showing.

   RESULT
	The requester, which is the request structure of that kind, or null
	for a kind it does not know or no memory.

   BEHAVIOR
	The structure is the library's: the program reads it and never writes
	it or frees it. It holds what the requester was answered with until
	the next `AslRequest` on it or until `FreeAslRequest`, so a requester
	asked twice opens where it was left - same place, same size, same
	drawer, file and pattern.

	A file requester starts with its three fields empty unless
	`ASLFR_InitialFile`, `ASLFR_InitialDrawer` or `ASLFR_InitialPattern`
	says otherwise, and with no place of its own, so the first showing is
	put in the middle of the screen it opens on. A device requester lists
	every device that says what it speaks unless `ASLDR_Speaks` names a
	command set, and starts on no unit unless `ASLDR_InitialDevice` names
	one.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: for memory.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The requester is the caller's to free with `FreeAslRequest`. Every
	pointer the tags give - the title, the patterns, the hooks - stays the
	caller's and must outlive the requester; the three text fields are
	copied into the requester's own buffers.

   NOTES
	The tags of the three kinds share their numbers where they mean the
	same thing, and have their own meanings where they do not, so the kind
	is what decides how a number is read.

   SEE ALSO
	`AslRequest`, `FreeAslRequest`

   EXAMPLES
	    const req: *asl.FileRequester = @ptrCast(@alignCast(ab.AllocAslRequest(asl.ASL_FileRequest, &.{
	        .{ .tag = asl.ASLFR_TitleText, .data = @intFromPtr("Open") },
	        .{ .tag = asl.ASLFR_InitialDrawer, .data = @intFromPtr("SYS:") },
	        .{},
	    }) orelse return));
	    defer ab.FreeAslRequest(req);
asl.library/AslRequest                                  asl.library/AslRequest

   NAME
	AslRequest -- A requester put up, and answered or given up.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AslRequest(ab: *AslBase, requester: *anyopaque, tags: ?[*]const TagItem) bool

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -28.

   INPUTS
	- `ab` - asl.library's base.
	- `requester` - what `AllocAslRequest` made.
	- `tags` - what to change about it for this showing; null to put it up
	  as it stands. They are the tags `AllocAslRequest` takes, and what
	  they set stays set for the requests after this one.

   RESULT
	True when the requester was answered, false when it was given up -
	the Cancel button, the close gadget, Ctrl-C - or could not be shown.
	With `ASLFR_ReplyPort`: true when it is up, false when it could not be
	started or is up already.

   BEHAVIOR
	The requester opens on the screen of `ASLFR_Window`, or on
	`ASLFR_Screen`, or on the public screen `ASLFR_PubScreenName` names,
	or on the default public screen; with `ASLFR_SleepWindow` the parent
	window shows the busy pointer and takes no input while it is up. It
	opens where it was left the time before, at the size it was left, so
	a program that asks twice asks in the same place.

	The call runs the requester on the caller's process and returns when
	the user has answered: it does not come back to the program in
	between, which is why a program with a window of its own hands one in
	(`ASLFR_Window`) rather than leaving it to look stopped.

	**Given a port** (`ASLFR_ReplyPort`) it runs the requester on a
	process of its own, which holds the library open while it runs, and
	returns at once. When the requester is answered or given up, its
	message (`asl.AslMessage`) goes to the port, saying which requester and
	whether it was answered; the program takes it with GetMsg and does not
	reply it. The process starts in the caller's current drawer, so a
	drawer named relative to it is found. `AbortAslRequest` takes it down
	before it is answered.

	A drawer is read while the requester is up rather than before it
	shows, so a drawer of some thousands of entries can be answered as
	soon as the wanted one is there. A device requester lists the units
	expansion.library's ObtainDeviceUnits answers for its command set,
	one line each - the device, the unit, and what the board calls it -
	and nothing is opened or loaded to find them.

	What it was answered with is in the request structure and holds until
	the next call on that requester or until `FreeAslRequest`.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: for input, for the screen, and for the drawer to be read;
	  with a port, only for the process to be started.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a Process, not a bare Task: it opens a window and reads a
	  drawer, or starts the process that does.

   OWNERSHIP
	Everything the requester makes is its own and goes at the next request
	or at `FreeAslRequest`. The locks of a multi-select answer are the
	library's: they are not to be unlocked by the program. A requester up
	on its own process is the library's until its message has arrived:
	the program reads nothing in it, asks nothing of it and does not free
	it, and the port, the window and the screen it was given stay until
	then.

   NOTES
	The screen mode requester is not built yet and answers false.

   SEE ALSO
	`AllocAslRequest`, `FreeAslRequest`, `AbortAslRequest`

   EXAMPLES
	    if (ab.AslRequest(req, &.{
	        .{ .tag = asl.ASLFR_TitleText, .data = @intFromPtr("Save as") },
	        .{ .tag = asl.ASLFR_DoSaveMode, .data = 1 },
	        .{},
	    })) save(req.drawer, req.file);

	    // Asked without waiting; the answer comes on `port`.
	    _ = ab.AslRequest(req, &.{
	        .{ .tag = asl.ASLFR_Window, .data = @intFromPtr(window) },
	        .{ .tag = asl.ASLFR_ReplyPort, .data = @intFromPtr(port) },
	        .{},
	    });
asl.library/FreeAslRequest                          asl.library/FreeAslRequest

   NAME
	FreeAslRequest -- A requester given back, with everything it was answered with.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FreeAslRequest(ab: *AslBase, requester: ?*anyopaque) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -24.

   INPUTS
	- `ab` - asl.library's base.
	- `requester` - what `AllocAslRequest` made; null does nothing.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	The request structure goes with it: every pointer the program read out
	of it - the file, the drawer, the pattern, the names of a multi-select
	answer and the locks with them - is gone once this returns, so a
	program that wants to keep an answer copies it first.

	A requester still up on a process of its own (`ASLFR_ReplyPort`) is
	left as it is, since its process is using it: the program takes it
	down with `AbortAslRequest`, waits for its message and frees it then.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: for memory to be given back.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: takes asl's requester lock, a spinlock, while the requester is
	  taken off the list.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	What the tags handed the requester stays the caller's and is not
	touched.

   BUGS
	A requester whose message has been sent but not yet taken off the
	port cannot be told from one that is the program's again: freed then,
	the message on the port is gone with it.

   SEE ALSO
	`AllocAslRequest`, `AslRequest`, `AbortAslRequest`

   EXAMPLES
	    ab.FreeAslRequest(req);
